---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

/* The three baptisms that look like the tree's chain, and the fathers that
   destroy it. Every one of these was read in the full transcription, not off
   the results list — which is the entire point of the page. */
const CHAIN = [
  ["Thomas", "17 Mar 1711/12", "Dursley St James", "Abraham", "Jane",
   "the tree wants a Thomas born 1711. Here he is, to the year — and his father is Abraham."],
  ["William", "6 Nov 1743", "Dursley St James", "John", "Mary",
   "the tree wants this William to be Thomas's son. His father is John."],
  ["William", "27 Sep 1770", "Dursley St James", "Samuel", "Phebe",
   "the tree wants this William to be the 1743 William's son. His father is Samuel."],
];

const COVERAGE = [
  ["Dursley", "St James", "Parish Register", "30,784", 1577, 1951, true],
  ["Cam", "St George", "Parish Register", "17,150", 1568, 1939, true],
  ["Thornbury", "St Mary", "Parish Register", "21,360", 1550, 1874, true],
  ["North Nibley", "St Martin", "Parish Register", "8,345", 1567, 1862, true],
  ["Berkeley", "St Mary", "Other Transcript", "2,916", 1600, 1677, false],
];

const BRISTOL = [
  ["Countess of Huntingdon's Chapel, Lodge Street", "Other Register", "81", "1820–1837"],
  ["Methodist Chapel, Bishopston", "Other Register", "98", "1891–1915"],
  ["Moravian Church, Maudlin Street", "Other Register", "1,016", "1755–1865"],
  ["Temple of Holy Cross", "Parish Register", "356", "1834–1849"],
];

const PLACES = [["Cam", 184], ["Dursley", 156], ["Thornbury", 55], ["Stone", 21],
                ["Berkeley", 18], ["Stinchcombe", 16], ["North Nibley", 6], ["Falfield", 5]];

const SPELLINGS = ["SAINGER", "SANIGER", "SANIGAR", "SANIGAER", "SINAGER", "SINNEGER",
                   "SINNEGAR", "SINEGAR", "SYNEGAR", "SYNAGER", "SINGER"];
---
<Base
  title="Berkeley, Gloucestershire"
  eyebrow="FreeREG and FreeBMD · searched 10 and 13 September 2026"
  dek="The oldest claim in this archive is a Gloucestershire yeoman born in 1654. Three baptisms match the tree's dates for the middle of that line. Opening them shows three different fathers.">

  <section class="blk" style="border-top:0">
    <Note style="border-left-width:3px;margin-bottom:0">
      <strong>Superseded in part, 13 September 2026.</strong> This page's central complaint — that
      FreeREG's Berkeley transcript stops in 1677 and therefore five of the tree's six Saniger
      generations cannot be tested — is no longer true. They can be tested, and they have been:
      Thomas baptised 1744, his marriage to Catherine Cotton in 1769, John baptised five weeks later,
      Thomas buried 1819 aged 75, John buried 1825 aged 55, and John's children in 1801, 1803 and
      1808. The records were behind a subscription, not behind a gap.
      <a href={u("/behind-the-paywall/")}>What they say →</a>
    </Note>
  </section>


  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">For as long as this archive has existed it has said its deepest documented person
    is <strong>William Sanigar, born 1654, of Berkeley in Gloucestershire</strong> — twelve
    generations back. An audit found that claim rested on
    <strong>other people's family trees and nothing else</strong>.
    <a href={u("/how-far-back/")}>The audit →</a> This page is what happened when the registers were
    searched properly.</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>The short answer, and it is worse than last time.</strong> The family is real and it is
      in the Vale in numbers. <strong>Three baptisms match the tree's dates almost exactly</strong> —
      a Thomas in 1711/12, a William in 1743, a William in 1770, all at Dursley. Read the full
      entries and their fathers are <strong>Abraham, John and Samuel</strong>. They are three
      unrelated men who happen to be baptised in the right parish in the right years.
      <strong>The chain is a coincidence of dates, and it does not survive being opened.</strong>
    </Note>
  </section>

  <Blk label="Correcting this page, 13 September 2026" title="It said these generations could not be tested. They could.">
    <p>The first version of this page reported that five of the six Saniger generations were
    untestable, because Berkeley St Mary's transcript stops in 1677. That was true of
    <em>Berkeley</em> and it was the wrong conclusion, because it treated one parish as the whole
    question.</p>
    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parish</th><th>Church</th><th>Register</th><th class="m">Records</th>
          <th class="m">First</th><th class="m">Last</th><th>Covers the chain?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {COVERAGE.map(([p, ch, ty, n, a, b, ok]) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{p}</strong></td><td>{ch}</td><td class="small">{ty}</td>
              <td class="m">{n}</td><td class="m">{a}</td><td class="m">{b}</td>
              <td>{ok ? <span class="chip doc">yes, entirely</span>
                      : <span class="chip lore">stops 132 years short</span>}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Dursley runs to 1951 and Cam to 1939.</strong> Both sit three
    to six miles from Berkeley in the same Vale, and between them they hold
    <strong>340 of the 494</strong> Saniger records this archive found for 1670–1830. The generations
    were testable all along, in the next parish.
    <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Recorded with the other errors →</a></p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="The test, and the result" title="Three baptisms, three fathers, no chain">
    <p>Searched across the whole county, every spelling, all record types: <strong>494 Saniger
    records between 1670 and 1830</strong>. Three of them land on the tree's dates.</p>

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Baptised</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>Parish</th><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {CHAIN.map(([who, when, where, fa, mo, note]) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{who}</strong><div class="small">{note}</div></td>
              <td class="m">{when}</td><td class="small">{where}</td>
              <td><strong>{fa}</strong></td><td>{mo}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <Note>
      <strong>This is the most useful thing on the page.</strong> Three baptisms in one parish,
      matching three dates in a pedigree, is exactly what a discovery looks like — and it is exactly
      what a records-matcher would have joined up and published. The difference between a finding and
      a fiction here was <strong>one click per record</strong>, to read a field the results list does
      not show you. <a href={u("/method/")}>How claims are labelled →</a>
    </Note>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>And the generation below them does not exist at all.</strong>
    The tree carries a Saniger born about 1805. There is <strong>no Saniger baptism anywhere in
    Gloucestershire between 1801 and 1809</strong> — and that is a real null, not a coverage gap,
    because Dursley and Cam are transcribed straight through it.
    <span class="chip lore">searched and empty</span></p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="The join this archive actually needs" title="Everything rests on one line in a Queensland death register">

    <p class="lede">The whole Saniger claim hangs on a single fact: when
    <a href={u("/people/miriam-wakefield/")}>Miriam Wakefield</a> died in Brisbane in 1909, the
    registrar wrote her parents down as <strong>James Wakefield and Hannah Saniger</strong>. Remove
    that line and the archive has no connection to Gloucestershire whatever.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">So the obvious thing is to find Hannah Saniger in England. She is
    not there.</p>

    <ul class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>No Hannah Saniger in Gloucestershire, 1780–1880.</strong>
      A fuzzy search across Gloucestershire, Somerset and Wiltshire returns 35 women and not one of
      them is in Gloucestershire — they are all Somerset and Wiltshire SINGERs and SANGERs that the
      phonetic match swept up. <span class="chip lore">searched and empty</span></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>No Saniger ever marries a Wakefield.</strong> In 139
      Gloucestershire records spanning 1790 to 1900 — the family is continuously there, at Cam,
      Dursley and Thornbury — the name Wakefield never appears.
      <span class="chip lore">searched and empty</span></li>
      <li><strong>And no such marriage was registered.</strong> Civil registration begins on 1 July
      1837. The earliest Saniger or Sanigar marriage in the Bristol district is
      <em>John Sanigar, March quarter 1846</em> — six years after Miriam was born. If her parents
      married, they married before July 1837, or somewhere else.
      <span class="chip lore">searched and empty</span></li>
    </ul>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Why none of that is proof of anything" title="Bristol is not in the database">
    <p>Before reporting any of those absences, the coverage was checked. It is as well it was.
    <strong>FreeREG's entire holding for the city of Bristol is four registers and 1,551
    records</strong> — for a city of well over a hundred thousand people.</p>

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Church</th><th>Register type</th><th class="m">Records</th><th class="m">Years</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {BRISTOL.map(([ch, ty, n, yrs]) => (
            <tr><td>{ch}</td><td class="small">{ty}</td><td class="m">{n}</td><td class="m">{yrs}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <Note>
      <strong>St Paul, Bristol — where the tree says Miriam was baptised — is not in that list.</strong>
      Neither is any other Bristol parish except Temple. So the marriage and the baptism this archive
      most needs are not absent from the record; they are absent from the <em>transcription</em>, and
      reporting them as null results would have been a straightforward lie.
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>Every source read, with which of the three this was →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="What civil registration did give up" title="Found: Miriam Wakefield, June quarter 1840">

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Event</td><td><strong>Birth registration</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Name</td><td><strong>Miriam Wakefield</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quarter</td><td class="m">June quarter 1840</td></tr>
          <tr><td>District</td><td><strong>Bristol</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reference</td><td class="m"><strong>volume 11, page 183</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Source</td><td>FreeBMD, the GRO index · searched 13 September 2026</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">She is the <strong>only Miriam Wakefield registered anywhere in
    England between 1839 and 1843</strong>. The tree says she was born on 11 April 1840 in Bristol; a
    birth on 11 April falls in the June quarter exactly as it should.
    <span class="chip doc">documented</span></p>

    <Note>
      <strong>And this is the way through.</strong> A GRO birth certificate names the mother
      <em>including her maiden surname</em>. One certificate — <span class="mono">Jun 1840, Bristol,
      vol 11 p 183</span>, £2.50 as a PDF — tests “Hannah Saniger” at the English end, which nothing
      else found so far can do. It is now the first item on the errands list.
      <a href={u("/errands/")}>The queue →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="A correction made mid-research, kept" title="The story that wasn't">
    <p>Reading the Berkeley list, an obvious and affecting sequence appeared: <em>Agnies Sainger
    buried at Berkeley in late May 1655; William baptised on the 24th of that month; Edward Sainger
    married Mary Smyth that July.</em> A mother dead in childbirth and a widower remarried within ten
    weeks.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem"><strong>It is not true.</strong> Opening the burial record shows
    Agnies entered as "<em>wife of</em>" with the surname <strong>Tyler</strong> — not a Sainger at
    all. It took one click to destroy, and it is the same click that destroyed the Dursley chain
    further up this page.</p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="The unexpected result, confirmed" title="They are not really a Berkeley family">
    <p>Of 494 Saniger records in the county between 1670 and 1830:</p>
    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parish</th><th class="m">Records</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {PLACES.map(([p, n]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{p}</strong></td><td class="m">{n}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Cam and Dursley hold 340 between them; Berkeley holds
    18.</strong></p>

    <Note>
      <strong>This page used to end that sentence by saying the archive should stop calling this a
      Berkeley family — that "of Berkeley" looked like a label somebody applied to the whole Vale.
      That was wrong, and interestingly wrong.</strong>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Saniger is a hamlet in Berkeley parish.</strong> The Berkeley Castle deeds name people
      "of Saniger" from before 1291; Bigland writes in 1791 that "Saniger [was] long held by an old
      Family of the same Name"; and an Edward Saniger of Saniger, Gent., stands as the freeholder for
      Hinton tithing at the election of 1776. The surname is taken off the land, which is why it
      concentrates in one Vale and why the eighteen Berkeley entries matter more than the three
      hundred and forty elsewhere: <em>Berkeley is where the name was made</em>, and Dursley and Cam
      are where the family went. <a href={u("/saniger-place/")}>The place, and its records →</a>
      <br /><br />
      The counts on this page are still right. The conclusion drawn from them was not.
    </Note>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>The spellings, all of them one family:</strong>{" "}
    {SPELLINGS.map((s, i) => (<>{i > 0 && " · "}<span class="mono">{s}</span></>))}.
    A search for "Saniger" alone finds a fraction of them — which is why
    <a href={u("/search/")}> this site's own search folds them together</a>. Reading all 556 records
    individually raises the count to <strong>seventeen</strong>, and the commonest form in the
    registers is not <em>Saniger</em> at all but <strong>SINNEGAR</strong>.
    <a href={u("/sanigers/")}>All seventeen, counted →</a>
    <a href={u("/name/")}> Why spelling is not identity →</a></p>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Where this leaves the oldest claim</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The family is real and it is in the right Vale.</strong>
      494 records between 1670 and 1830, continuing unbroken to 1900.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The middle of the chain is now actively contradicted,
      not merely untested.</strong> The three baptisms that fit the tree's dates have three different
      fathers. <span class="chip sup">the chain, as the tree draws it, fails</span></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The 1805 generation is not there at all</strong>, in a
      window that is thoroughly transcribed. <span class="chip lore">searched and empty</span></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The 1654 Berkeley candidate still stands, and still
      cannot be identified.</strong> William Sainger, baptised 24 May 1655, son of Edward Sainger of
      Wanswell — one of three Williams, each the son of an Edward.
      <span class="chip inf">candidate, not identification</span></li>
      <li><strong>The join to this family is untested and now has a price.</strong> Hannah Saniger
      cannot be found in England; Bristol is not transcribed; and one £2.50 certificate would settle
      whether she existed under that name at all.</li>
    </ol>

    <Note>
      <strong>The archive's floor has not moved, but the ceiling has come down.</strong> This is still
      about seven generations of genuinely evidenced ancestry. What has changed is that the storey
      above it is no longer merely unproven — <strong>parts of it have now been looked for and found
      to be wrong</strong>. <a href={u("/how-far-back/")}>How far back it really goes →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/errands/")}>The certificate that would settle it →</a>
    </Note>
  </section>
</Base>
